Feature Release Timeline
•	2017 — NOMI Insights + NOMI Find & Save: AI-driven spending insights and automated savings launched as Canada's first such mobile banking features.
•	2019 — NOMI Budgets: First fully AI-driven budgeting tool in Canadian banking; customers opened 1.2M new budgets in Year 1.
•	2020 — Ask NOMI: Conversational chatbot supporting 12 languages, built on Personetics Assist; answered 1M+ questions in first year.
•	2021 — NOMI Forecast: Deep learning cashflow prediction (7-day horizon) — the first Borealis AI model ever deployed into a retail banking product.
Frontend (Mobile UI)
All NOMI features were delivered as native experiences within the existing RBC Mobile app (iOS and Android), requiring new UI components, flows, and notification systems for each release.
•	NOMI Insights: Real-time spending cards surfaced via push notifications and in-app nudges, designed for one-tap dismissal or drill-down. Required building a dynamic card rendering system capable of displaying dozens of AI-generated insight types.
•	NOMI Find & Save: One-tap savings approval UI with transfer confirmation flows and savings history tracking. Integrated with the savings account backend via new REST endpoints.
•	NOMI Budgets: Multi-step budget creation wizard (AI-recommended defaults + user customization), category editors, real-time spend vs. budget ring charts, and weekly push alerts. A/B tested notification formats showed 40% higher opt-in rates for personalized alerts.
•	Ask NOMI: Conversational chat interface embedded in the app — new chat bubble UI, natural language input, multi-language rendering (12 languages), and voice input integration.
•	NOMI Forecast: 7-day cashflow timeline view showing upcoming preauthorized payments as cards on a scrollable calendar strip. Tappable entries with payment detail overlays. Led to 10M+ interactions since launch.
Backend (APIs & Microservices)
NOMI required a full greenfield backend layer — decoupling transaction data from the mainframe, building new microservices for each feature, and exposing clean APIs for the mobile clients.
•	Mainframe Offload: Transaction and profile read workloads were migrated off the mainframe into a cloud-native microservice layer. The mainframe had been high-read/low-write, creating performance bottlenecks for real-time personalization — this migration was foundational to enabling NOMI at scale.
•	Microservices Architecture: Each NOMI feature (Insights, Find & Save, Budgets, Ask NOMI, Forecast) was backed by a dedicated microservice — independently deployable, versioned, and owned by a feature team.
•	API Platform: RBC's internal API platform (awarded the 2019 Digital Transformation Award) processed 1.5B transactions/month and onboarded ~800 APIs, forming the integration backbone between NOMI services and core banking systems. The platform generated $20M in savings.
•	REST + Event APIs: New REST endpoints exposed NOMI data to the mobile apps; internal event APIs connected to Kafka topics for downstream ML model ingestion and inference serving.
•	Data Unification: Customer transaction data from 29 countries was unified into a central data lake, solving data silo problems that had previously made cross-product personalization impossible.
Infrastructure & DevOps (Kafka, OpenShift, Cloud)
NOMI's scale demands — millions of users, real-time AI inference, streaming transaction data — required a ground-up infrastructure transformation running in parallel with feature delivery.
•	Apache Kafka / Confluent Platform: RBC adopted Confluent Platform as the event streaming backbone. A microservice was written specifically to intercept mainframe reads and publish them as Kafka events instead, dramatically reducing mainframe load. 50+ applications were eventually deployed on this event-driven architecture.
•	OpenShift (Kubernetes) GPU Cluster: NOMI was RBC's first production workload on Borealis AI's OpenShift GPU cluster. CPU-only infrastructure was infeasible for NOMI's deep learning inference latency requirements — the GPU cluster, using Red Hat OpenShift as the orchestration layer, enabled the transition from research to production for ML models.
•	AWS Cloud Adoption: RBC adopted Amazon EC2 for application hosting and computing in 2021, with NOMI services among the early cloud-native workloads. Cloud infrastructure enabled elastic scaling to handle peak transaction volumes.
•	CI/CD Pipelines: Automated build, test, and deployment pipelines were built to support the high release cadence across five NOMI features and multiple mobile platforms simultaneously.
•	Federated Learning Infrastructure: To comply with Canadian privacy regulations (OSFI), customer data was handled via federated learning techniques — model training was distributed across data partitions without centralizing raw PII.
ML / AI (Models & Pipelines)
NOMI's ML stack evolved across two distinct layers — the Personetics Engage AI platform (features 1–4) and the proprietary Borealis AI deep learning layer (Forecast). Engineers interfaced with both, building the data pipelines and serving infrastructure that connected models to production.
•	Personetics Engage AI Platform (Insights, Find & Save, Budgets, Ask NOMI): Transaction categorization, anomaly detection, surplus detection ($50–$500 range auto-identified), and budget recommendation models. Find & Save helped clients save an average of $415/month; the feature drove 2B+ personalized insights read since launch.
•	Borealis AI Deep Learning (NOMI Forecast — 2021): A deep learning model trained on historical transaction data to predict upcoming payment dates and amounts. Covered pre-authorized payments, money transfers, cheques, and e-transfers. Delivered 85% prediction accuracy on 30-day cashflow horizon; served 1.7M+ predictions since launch.
•	Model Serving Architecture: ML/AI systems were decoupled from product features via well-defined API contracts — Borealis AI owned model training and production operation; product engineering teams consumed model outputs through versioned interfaces. This enabled each team to iterate independently.
•	Training Data Pipelines: Kafka event streams fed transaction data into model training pipelines on the OpenShift GPU cluster. Historical data from the unified data lake provided the training corpus for all models.
•	Explainability: All NOMI models were required to produce explainable outputs — a non-negotiable for regulatory compliance and client trust. This shaped model architecture choices across the platform.
