Platform Evolution
•	2016–2017 — Concept & R&D: Borealis AI and RBC Capital Markets equities desk began exploring deep reinforcement learning for order execution. Whiteboarding, simulation environments, and academic collaboration.
•	2017–2019 — Lab to Live: First beta trades executed internally. Actor-critic architecture validated in live but controlled market conditions.
•	2020 — Global Launch (Aiden VWAP): Platform launched for US and Canadian equities clients. VWAP (Volume-Weighted Average Price) was the first live algorithm, making 200+ input calculations per order in real time. Notably, Aiden held performance through COVID-19 market volatility in early 2020 — a major stress test that predictive AIs failed.
•	2021 — European Expansion: Platform extended to European equities markets, requiring new market data feeds, regulatory compliance layers, and localized execution rules.
•	2022 — Aiden Arrival (Second Algorithm): A new algorithm targeting Arrival Price benchmark, increasing inputs from 200 to 300 and expanding the action space for finer execution control.
Frontend (Trading UI & Explainability Dashboards)
Trading platforms require low-latency, high-density UIs. Aiden added a new layer of complexity: traders needed to understand and trust AI decisions, not just see execution results.
•	Order Management UI: Client-facing and internal trader interfaces for order submission, real-time status tracking, and execution monitoring. Built to support 8,000+ users across RBC Capital Markets globally.
•	Explainability Dashboards: A regulatory and trust requirement — dashboards showing traders which market signals Aiden acted on, how aggressive it was at each time step, and how its choices compared to benchmark. Translated RL model outputs (softmax distributions, value function estimates) into human-readable trading rationale.
•	Post-Execution Reports: After each order, clients received structured reports breaking down Aiden's trading actions, slippage vs. benchmark, and market context at key decision points.
•	European Localization: UI adaptations for European market conventions, currency formatting, and regulatory disclosure requirements when the platform expanded in 2021.
Backend (Order Execution Engine & Data Infrastructure)
Aiden's backend is one of the most demanding engineering environments possible — real-time, latency-sensitive, financially regulated, and processing millions of calculations per second.
•	Order Execution Engine: The core backend service responsible for receiving client orders, passing them to the Aiden RL model for action selection, converting model output into limit orders, and submitting them to the live limit order book — all within microsecond latency targets.
•	Real-Time Market Data Feeds: Aiden ingests live market data (order book depth, volume, price, volatility signals) at each time step. The backend normalized and routed data from multiple exchange feeds into a unified format consumable by the model.
•	32M+ Calculations Per Order: Each order spawned a real-time inference loop — at every time step, 200+ market features were processed through the deep neural network to produce an action distribution. The backend managed this compute loop end-to-end, including guardrails to cap aggressiveness within risk limits.
•	External Data Integration: Through a single API platform, RBC integrated licensed and normalized external market data directly into Aiden, replacing years of bespoke data engineering and vendor management work.
•	Multi-Region Deployment: Separate backend instances were deployed for US, Canadian, and European equities markets, each with region-specific market data feeds, execution rules, and compliance checks.
•	Risk & Guardrail Layer: A rule-based guardrail system sat between the RL model and the market, enforcing hard limits on order aggressiveness, position sizing, and deviation from benchmark — required by both internal risk management and regulators.
Infrastructure & DevOps (AI Factory, Hybrid Cloud)
Aiden required an entirely new class of compute infrastructure — GPU-accelerated, hybrid cloud, low-latency, and production-grade for a safety-critical financial system.
•	NVIDIA AI Factory: RBC Capital Markets built a dedicated AI factory using NVIDIA Accelerated Computing to support Aiden's training and inference workloads — combining on-premise GPU clusters with hybrid cloud solutions for elasticity.
•	OpenShift Orchestration: Aiden's ML workloads were containerized and orchestrated on Red Hat OpenShift, the same GPU cluster used for NOMI Forecast. OpenShift managed CPU+GPU resource scheduling across research and production environments.
•	Simulation Environment: Before any model touched live markets, a full market simulation environment was built and maintained — allowing new RL algorithm versions (VWAP → Arrival → future algorithms) to be trained, validated, and tested in replay before deployment.
•	Model Versioning & Rollout: Given the financial stakes, model versioning and staged rollout infrastructure was critical. New algorithm versions (e.g., Aiden Arrival in 2022) required parallel running, shadow mode testing, and phased client migration.
•	Monitoring & Alerting: Real-time performance monitoring tracked slippage vs. benchmark, model confidence (value function outputs), and execution anomalies — with automated alerts for deviation outside acceptable ranges.
•	Databricks Data Pipeline: RBC used Databricks to merge structured financial data with unstructured text in real time, validating every figure before it entered Aiden's data pipeline — ensuring data quality for a system where bad inputs directly cause financial losses.
ML / AI (Deep Reinforcement Learning Architecture)
Aiden is technically one of the most sophisticated applied RL systems in production finance. Its architecture was purpose-built for partially observable, non-stationary environments — the hallmark of live equities markets.
•	Actor-Critic Architecture: Aiden uses a recurrent actor-critic neural network. The actor outputs a softmax distribution over a discrete action space (order aggressiveness levels); the critic outputs a scalar value function estimating expected future reward from the current state.
•	Recurrent Layer (LSTM): Because financial markets are partially observable — Aiden can never see the complete market state — a recurrent layer was added to propagate information across time steps. At each step, Aiden takes market features, self-aware features (its own recent behaviour), and the recurrent vector from the previous step as input, and outputs the next action distribution plus an updated recurrent vector.
•	Market Features (200+ inputs for VWAP, 300+ for Arrival): Inputs included real-time order book depth, trade volume, spread, volatility metrics, time-of-day signals, and order-specific context. Aiden Arrival extended the input space to capture arrival price dynamics.
•	Self-Aware Features: Aiden tracked its own recent execution behaviour as part of its state — how aggressively it had been trading, how much of the order remained, and how its recent actions performed — enabling self-correcting behaviour mid-order.
•	Reward System: The reward function was designed to penalize slippage against benchmark (VWAP or Arrival Price) while balancing market impact. This required careful reward engineering to prevent Aiden from learning degenerate strategies that minimized immediate slippage at the cost of overall execution quality.
•	COVID-19 Stress Test (2020): Traditional predictive trading AIs failed during the extreme volatility of March 2020. Aiden's RL approach — adapting to observed market conditions rather than relying on historical predictions — allowed it to continue performing close to benchmark during one of the most volatile periods in modern market history.
•	Continuous Learning: Aiden improved with every order it processed. Unlike traditional algorithms that required manual re-coding as market conditions changed, Aiden's RL framework allowed it to adapt within guardrails — learning from rewards and penalties in real time.
